<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Friedberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1211 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Friedberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Wetterau imperial city that shared its hill with a powerful imperial castle, whose knights gradually took control of the town.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperors built a castle and founded a town at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedberg-hesse">Friedberg</a> on a ridge in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetterau">Wetterau</a>, the town being documented as an imperial city in 1211. It lay on the trade route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> to the north and held important fairs in the 13th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city council governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a>, but the castle's <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/burgraviate-of-friedberg">Burgraviate of Friedberg</a> next door constantly competed with it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedberg-hesse">Friedberg</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetterau">Wetterau</a> city league with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-wetzlar">Wetzlar</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-gelnhausen">Gelnhausen</a>. Cloth making and trade made it prosperous until the 14th century, and its Jewish community built a deep ritual bath around 1260 that survives. Debts and decline later left the town largely under the castle's influence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The city was pledged in 1455 and gradually dominated by the Burgraviate. It adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> and kept nominal imperial status until 1802, when it passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Hesse-Darmstadt</a>. It is now a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-friedberg.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Friedberg" width="320" height="243"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedberg_(Hessen)_Stadtkirche.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1211 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Friedberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Friedberg (Hessen)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1506&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-friedberg" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Friedberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4880 429 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Friedberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5200 685L5229 694M5200 685L5198 698"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5198 698h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5200 685h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5200 685h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1506&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-friedberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
